      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 109,
      "player": "Llama4 Johnny",
      "decision_index": 100,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Grok4F ESPN responded to their own Thoughtseize (on the stack, targeting Llama4 Johnny) by casting March of Otherworldly Light to exile Psychic Frog. This is poor sequencing - Thoughtseize would resolve first, letting ESPN see Llama4 Johnny's hand and potentially take a key card (like a counterspell or threat). By casting March now in response to their own spell, they spend mana and a card before getting the information from Thoughtseize. Additionally, March of Otherworldly Light for X=1 costs {1}{W}, but they could have cast it after Thoughtseize resolved, using the hand information to decide whether the Frog was even the right target or if there was a bigger threat to worry about. Furthermore, by using March here they're spending mana that could be relevant for Force of Negation backup or other plays. The Psychic Frog is a real threat worth removing, but the sequencing wastes the information advantage from Thoughtseize.",
      "action_taken": "Cast March of Otherworldly Light (X=1) to exile Psychic Frog while Thoughtseize was still on the stack",
      "better_line": "Let Thoughtseize resolve first to see opponent's hand, then decide whether to use March on Psychic Frog or save removal for a bigger threat revealed in hand",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 113,
      "player": "Grok4F ESPN",
      "decision_index": 109,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Opponent cast Thoughtseize targeting Grok4F ESPN. Grok4F ESPN has Force of Negation in hand and it's not their turn (wait - actually it IS their turn, turn 9 belongs to Grok4F ESPN). But looking more carefully: the Thoughtseize is on the stack, and Grok4F ESPN is responding. Since it's Grok4F ESPN's own turn, Force of Negation cannot be cast for its alternate cost (exile a blue card). Force of Negation costs 1UU to hardcast, and ESPN has 4 lands tapped out of 6 (Shadowy Backstreet, Island, Watery Grave, Plains are tapped; Hallowed Fountain and Godless Shrine are untapped). Hallowed Fountain produces W or U, Godless Shrine produces W or B - that's only 2 mana available, not enough for 1UU. So Force of Negation can't be cast here. The choices given are Force of Negation and three Faithful Mendings (flashback from graveyard). Wait - but actually Thoughtseize is a sorcery, so if it's being cast on ESPN's turn... Actually, looking at the board state, Llama4 Johnny has tapped lands too. Let me reconsider - the Thoughtseize on the stack was likely cast by Llama4 Johnny on a previous turn's resolution, or ESPN cast it targeting themselves? No - ESPN is the active player and the message says 'Play instants and activated abilities' in response to Thoughtseize on the stack. ESPN chose Faithful Mending (flashback) instead of letting Thoughtseize resolve. Faithful Mending costs 1WU to flashback. With Hallowed Fountain (W/U) and Godless Shrine (W/B) untapped, ESPN can produce WU or WW or WB - not enough for 1WU (needs 3 mana, only 2 lands untapped). Actually wait, the choice was offered so presumably it's legal. The real question is whether responding with Faithful Mending to Thoughtseize is correct. By casting Faithful Mending, ESPN draws 2 cards and discards 2, gaining 2 life, BEFORE Thoughtseize resolves. This gives the opponent more information and more cards to choose from when Thoughtseize resolves. It would have been better to let Thoughtseize resolve first (opponent sees current hand and takes a card), then flashback Faithful Mending afterward to potentially draw replacements. By casting Mending first, opponent gets to see the refreshed hand including new draws.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Faithful Mending (flashback) in response to Thoughtseize, drawing 2 cards and discarding 2 before Thoughtseize resolves.",
      "better_line": "Let Thoughtseize resolve first, allowing the opponent to see only the current hand (Atraxa x2, Force of Negation, Thoughtseize). After Thoughtseize takes a card, then flashback Faithful Mending to draw fresh cards. This way the opponent has less information and fewer options to choose from with Thoughtseize.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 116,
      "player": "Grok4F ESPN",
      "decision_index": 116,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Discarded Thoughtseize instead of the second Force of Negation. Thoughtseize is efficient 1-mana proactive disruption that's easy to cast. The second Force of Negation would be difficult to cast for its alternate cost since the only blue card remaining in hand would be Atraxa (their win condition they don't want to exile). Hard-casting Force at 1UU is also demanding. Thoughtseize provides more practical utility in the current game state.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Thoughtseize and Atraxa, Grand Unifier, keeping both Forces of Negation and Solitude",
      "better_line": "Discard the second Force of Negation and Atraxa, Grand Unifier, keeping Thoughtseize for cheap proactive disruption alongside one Force of Negation and Solitude",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 117,
