      "decision_index": 137,
    },
    {
      "severity": "questionable",
      "description": "Discarding Force of Negation and Psychic Frog while keeping Ephemerate and Atraxa. The opponent (Llama4 Johnny) has Emperor of Bones on the battlefield and multiple high-value reanimation targets in their graveyard including Archon of Cruelty and Abhorrent Oculus. Force of Negation is one of the few ways to interact with the opponent's spells on their turn for free, and all of Grok4F ESPN's lands are tapped. While keeping Atraxa as a potential Goryo's Vengeance target or hard cast makes sense, discarding both copies of Force of Negation (one here, potentially drawing into needing them later) leaves Grok4F ESPN vulnerable. However, since all lands are tapped and this is during combat on Grok4F ESPN's own turn, the defensive value of Force of Negation is somewhat diminished in the immediate term. Keeping Ephemerate to rebuy ETB triggers is reasonable. This is debatable but losing both free counterspells when the opponent has threatening lines is concerning.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Force of Negation and Psychic Frog to Faithful Mending, keeping Force of Negation, Ephemerate, and Atraxa, Grand Unifier.",
      "better_line": "Could consider discarding Ephemerate and Psychic Frog instead, keeping both Forces of Negation to protect against the opponent's reanimation spells and other threats. With Emperor of Bones threatening to reanimate Archon of Cruelty, having free counterplay is valuable.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 135,
      "player": "Grok4F ESPN",
      "decision_index": 138,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Emperor of Bones triggers at the beginning of combat, exiling a card from a graveyard, and when it gets +1/+1 counters (via Adapt 2 for 1B), it puts a creature exiled with it onto the battlefield with haste and a finality counter. Exiling Gran-Gran (a 1/2) is far weaker than exiling Archon of Cruelty (a 6/6 with a devastating ETB trigger that makes the opponent sacrifice a creature/planeswalker, discard a card, lose 3 life, and draws you a card and gains 3 life) or Atraxa, Grand Unifier (a 7/7 with flying, vigilance, deathtouch, lifelink and a powerful ETB). When Emperor of Bones adapts, bringing back Archon of Cruelty or Atraxa even for one turn would be game-changing, while Gran-Gran provides minimal value as a 1/2 that draws/discards when tapped.",
      "action_taken": "Exiled Gran-Gran (a 1/2) with Emperor of Bones' triggered ability",
