      "better_line": "Fetch Raucous Theater for the same B/R mana production plus a free surveil 1 to help find answers while at 2 life with an empty hand",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 167,
      "player": "Llama4 Johnny",
      "decision_index": 185,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "At 2 life facing an opponent at 29 life with a developed board, the player discarded Abhorrent Oculus (a 5/5 flyer for 3 mana that generates tokens each turn) and kept Ragavan (a 2/1 that's poor when behind and can't profitably attack into Superior Spider-Man with reach). Abhorrent Oculus was castable next turn with 14 cards in graveyard and was by far the most impactful card to stabilize from a losing position.",
      "action_taken": "Discarded Spell Pierce and Abhorrent Oculus, keeping Ragavan",
      "better_line": "Discard Spell Pierce and Ragavan, keeping Abhorrent Oculus as the best chance to stabilize with a 5/5 flyer that generates board presence",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 171,
      "player": "Llama4 Johnny",
      "decision_index": 190,
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "At 2 life facing a 4/4 Superior Spider-Man, casting Ragavan (a 2/1 that can't attack profitably into the 4/4) is suboptimal when Emperor of Bones offers better defensive and offensive utility. Emperor of Bones can exile powerful creatures from the opponent's graveyard (like Atraxa or Solitude) and potentially reanimate them. Additionally, both creatures could have been cast in the same turn using all 4 available mana, or Faithless Looting flashback could have been used to dig for critical answers at 2 life.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Ragavan, Nimble Pilferer first/alone",
      "better_line": "Cast Emperor of Bones (2 mana) first to set up graveyard exile triggers, then also cast Ragavan (1 mana) with remaining mana, deploying both threats. Alternatively, flashback Faithless Looting to dig for removal/answers at 2 life.",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 178,
      "player": "Llama4 Johnny",
      "decision_index": 196,
    },
    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "At 2 life with only Emperor of Bones in hand, Llama4 Johnny chose to Dash Ragavan for {1}{R} instead of casting it normally for {R}. Dashing costs an extra mana (using Blood Crypt + Island instead of just Blood Crypt) and returns Ragavan to hand at end of turn, meaning it won't stick as a blocker. At 2 life against an opponent with Superior Spider-Man (a 4/4) and potential attacks, having a permanent blocker could be critical for survival. The 'dash benefit' of haste is irrelevant since Ragavan has no summoning sickness issues for attacking this turn anyway — wait, Ragavan doesn't have haste naturally, so Dash does grant haste. However, spending 2 mana instead of 1 leaves less mana open, and more importantly, Ragavan bounces back to hand at end of turn, so it can't block next turn. At 2 life, a permanent 2/1 body that can block is far more valuable than the tempo of dashing, especially when you also want to cast Emperor of Bones ({1}{B}) and may not have mana for both. By dashing for {1}{R}, you use Blood Crypt + Island, leaving Swamp + Undercity Sewers, which is enough for Emperor of Bones. But next turn you'd need to recast Ragavan again. Casting normally for {R} saves mana and keeps the body on board as a potential blocker.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Ragavan with Dash for {1}{R}, giving it haste but requiring it to return to hand at end of turn",
      "better_line": "Cast Ragavan normally for {R}, saving mana and keeping it on the battlefield permanently as a potential blocker at a critical 2 life total",
      "type": "blunder",
      "snapshot_index": 179,
      "player": "Llama4 Johnny",
      "decision_index": 198,
    }
  ],
